  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 149 AM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nStrong northerly winds have been gusting up around 45-55 mph \novernight as a stronger winds aloft are pushed to the surface by \ncold air advecting into central and northeastern SD. These winds \nwill slowly be decreasing from west to east through the early \nmorning, with wind gusts over northeastern SD decreasing below 30 \nmph by mid morning. The stronger winds aloft have also been able to \ninteract with some moisture aloft and cause some scattered showers \nto develop overnight and move through the area. As the winds die \nback early this morning, so does the ability for these showers to \ndevelop. Through the day, drier air aloft will be moving in as high \nsurface pressure shifts over SD. Both the drier air and high \npressure will help to finally erode the clouds that have been stuck \nover central and northeastern SD and west central MN, and we will \nhave some clear skies by the afternoon. \n\nThose clear skies will help to cool temperatures tonight into \nSaturday morning into the mid to low 30s. These cold temperatures \ncould help frost to form over northeastern SD and west central MN \ntonight. This frost will have to form during the early overnight \nhours, because as the high pressure moves out this evening, strong \nsoutherly winds will move in over the area. These stronger winds \nwill disrupt the frost formation, and can keep the frost from \nforming into early Saturday morning. These winds will be gusting up \naround 25-30mph across central and northeastern SD overnight. \nAdditionally, as these winds reach the Prairie Coteau, they could \nbecome stronger due to downsloping winds. There is a 30-50% chance \nfor wind gusts to be greater than 45 mph along the eastern edge of \nthe coteau Saturday morning. A wind advisory might be needed for \nthese areas into early Saturday afternoon because of this. Warmer \ntemperatures will be moving in Saturday from the southerly winds, \nwhich will help to warm temperatures about 5-15 degrees warmer than \nnormal for this time of the year Saturday and Sunday.\n\nThe downsloping winds from the southwesterly direction will continue \ninto Saturday night. Then, a surface trough will then be moving \nthrough SD Sunday, shifting the winds to be coming from the north \nand decreasing the downsloping effects along the coteau. The drier \nair that moves in today will stick around through the beginning of \nthe week and will help to keep precipitation from developing as the \nsurface trough move through on Sunday, as well as into the beginning \nof the work week.",
